@@ -0,0 +1,13 @@
+---
+"@runfusion/fusion": patch
+---
+
+summary: A wake comment is announced to an agent once instead of on every wake.
+category: fix
+dev: STAS-330. The comment half of a heartbeat wake was a pure recomputation — every wake re-derived "which comments are newer than my last run" from the card, and nothing anywhere recorded which ids an earlier wake had already put in front of this agent for this card, so an id could be announced forever (measured on the deployed board: `project.agent_runs` carries 52,331 rows and exactly 1 of them ever recorded `contextSnapshot.triggeringCommentIds`, so there was no set to subtract). On the reported card four ids rode 15+ re-wakes in two hours and none of them could be resolved, because the advertised ids were hex-8 while every comment id on the board is `<epoch-ms>-<6 chars>` (12,022 ids measured, 0 of the advertised shape) — which is why the block read "0 of 0 readable".
+
+`agents/wake-comment-delta.ts` now owns the rule. `consumeWakeCommentDelta` reads the already-persisted advertisement off this agent's own prior run rows (`contextSnapshot.triggeringCommentIds`, falling back to `contextSnapshot.taskId` for the card link because top-level `data.taskId` exists on only 10 of 52,331 rows) and subtracts it at the wake's **fire time** inside `executeHeartbeat` — the single path every wake source flows through — so a deferred/re-fired `drainPendingAssignment` wake loses its stale id list at the same seam instead of replaying what it captured at schedule time, a crashed or terminated run cannot un-consume what it was told, and a wake left with nothing new still runs with no comment attribution. The remembered set is bounded by `WAKE_COMMENT_CONSUMPTION_SCAN_RUNS` (20) and `MAX_CONSUMED_WAKE_COMMENT_IDS` (200, newest-first); an id whose body is not on the card is still counted as advertised, so the loud "body is NOT on this card" line is said once rather than on every tick. Consumption is write-side only: `renderTaskCommentSection` / `fn_task_show(commentIds=…)` still return a consumed id's body, verified by test.
+
+Self-echo is the other half. A comment whose author string names the agent being woken is now withheld from its own wake delta (named on one `[wake-comment-delta]` log line, id still consumed), from the executor's `## Steering Comments` section (`buildSteeringCommentsSection`, extracted from `buildExecutionPrompt` so the rule is testable), from the step-session prompt (`buildStepSteeringCommentsSection`, which had become a second unfiltered copy of the rule at three call sites), and from both workflow-input reply detectors (`workflowInputRepliesAfterWatermark`, which `await-input-node` used to re-implement independently) — all of them through the one exported `isSelfAuthoredComment` predicate, so no second "is this new to me" exists. Suppression requires a positive author match and an unknown author is delivered as external input: on the deployed board 7,857 of 7,907 steering rows carry the two-value literal `author: "agent"` written by planner-oversight lifecycle guidance addressed *to* the executing agent, so filtering on `"agent"` would silence real instruction — a worse failure than one more echo. The filter is load-bearing where the delta actually reads from: the lookup is built from `taskDetail.comments` first, and 946 comment rows there carry an author outside the literal, 188+ of them a literal agent id. Compound author strings (`agent-5683bf15 (Engineer, heartbeat)`, 6 rows) deliberately do not match — exact equality only — because card text names foreign agent ids routinely and substring matching would suppress somebody else's instruction.
+
+Deliberately untouched: the message lane (STAS-307's `message-wake-idempotency` vocabulary — its markers are absent from this base, so the shared module keeps its own name and the merge order is recorded in the task handoff), STAS-300's wake cursor (a timer gate over a different population written by a different function; folding the two is the defect `heartbeat-wake-signals.ts` exists to prevent), the mid-run steering injection in `wire-executor-lifecycle.ts` (already exactly-once per session, and no agent-callable tool writes steering rows), and the read-side resolver (STAS-316). Two measurement verdicts are negative and shipped no code: `fn_task_log` has no `commentId` parameter and `logEntryImpl` writes no comment row, and no `[Turn N]` chain-replay label producer exists anywhere in the repo. `TRIGGERING_COMMENT_HEADING` is exported from the new module and imported by `heartbeat-prompt-trim.ts`, which locates the block by exact line match — the two copies of that literal were a drift risk.
